Subject: winealsa: report a device interface and Windows names for USB MIDI ports

Windows gives each USB MIDI port a name from its USB MIDI class driver. The
first port of a device gets the device name. The other ports get the names
"MIDIIN<n> (device)" and "MIDIOUT<n> (device)". Windows also answers
midi{In,Out}Message(DRV_QUERYDEVICEINTERFACE) with the KS device interface
path of the port. This path contains the USB ids of the device:

  \\?\usb#vid_2982&pid_1969&mi_05#7&1e0d0a41&0&0005#{6994ad04-...}

Applications use the names and the path to find their hardware. Ableton Live
looks for vid_xxxx&pid_xxxx in the path of each MIDI device. Live starts its
Push helper only if it finds these ids. The helper then compares the port
names with one pattern for each Push generation.

Wine answered this message with MMSYSERR_NOTSUPPORTED. The wave devices in
winmm answer the message, but the MIDI drivers did not. Wine also made the
port names from the ALSA client and port. Live therefore did not find a
Push 3, and it did not start Push3.exe.

winealsa now makes the path and the names from the USB device that is behind
the sound card of the port. The device link of the card in sysfs points to
the USB device. The USB device gives idVendor, idProduct and the number of
the MIDI streaming interface. The seq port number gives the Windows port
index. A port that has no USB card keeps the name "client - port" and reports
no path, as before.

midiInMessage must also accept a device ID for the physical-feature messages.
midiOutMessage calls MMDRV_PhysicalFeatures if the handle is an ID, but the
input function returned MMSYSERR_INVALHANDLE. Applications ask both
directions for the path.
